      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The estimate is the weighted average score for all 36 tests. We estimate the mean of the final score by first finding the exact mean of the first 30 tests, then the approximate mean of the last 6 tests, and using these means as weights in a weighted average formula. Clearly, the weighted average mean (or new class mean) must be closer to the weighted average means from the 2 tests than to either $72\\%$ or $78\\%$. To do this weighted average, we multiply the initial mean times the initial sample size and the target mean times the target size, then divide the sum by $36$.\nTo find our estimate of the new class mean, we start by finding the exact mean of the 30 initial tests, and the approximate mean (the weighted average of the two final samples), *then* finding the weighted average using both as weights. We have the mean of this weighted average is lower than both averages given, confirming this will be the new class mean. We see that 18 students took the test, but their average is only 72%, giving us a base weight for the entire test of $72\\%$. To this we add the weighted average score of the last 6 students:\n$$\n\\frac{18\\cdot 72 + 6\\cdot 78}{36} = \\frac{1296 + 468}{36} = \\frac{1764}{36} = 49\\%\n\\quality{steps}{}\n$$\n\nSolution:\nWhat is an estimate of the new class mean can be found by a weighted average of two different means of equal sizes by appropriately weighting these means by the size of the two different means. This acheives the same outcome as the solution above, but in a shorter space of words. Adding the students together and first getting the average of the different classes gives a weight of $\\dfrac16+{\\dfrac{1}{3}}=\\dfrac12$. Plugging in this value into the formula yields $\\dfrac{30\\cdot 72 + 6\\cdot 78}{6+3}=\\dfrac{2160+468}{6+3}=\\dfrac{2628}{36}=\\boxed{73\\%}$.  [*Note that if we did this the contrarly, perhaps first taking the average of each $\\frac12$, we would get $\\dfrac{\\frac{\\frac{30}{6}\\cdot 72}{1}}{\\frac{3}{2}+1}=\\boxed{73\\%}$ in the end. However, we usually do the exact opposite)*].\nNoticing where 6 and 36 equal factors of ${\\dfrac{6+3}}={\\dfrac{9}}$ leads to a shortcut.\n$\\boxed{\\dfrac{{18}\\cdot 72+{6}\\cdot 78}{{9}}}$\n\\[73+\\frac{76}{9} = \\boxed{73.7777...}\\",
